course: dictionary
lesson: 6866
title: Words with two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Overdose» is one of them. Its serious meaning is too much medicine or drug taken at once, which can make someone very ill or even kill them.
explain: Read this: «Even shop medicines such as cough medicine can be dangerous in big amounts, and any overdose (too much medicine) is an emergency.» The brackets give you a clue, and the word emergency tells you it is serious.
explain: Overdose also has a joking meaning: too much of something, like «an overdose of homework». Nobody is ill here. Ask yourself: is this about medicine, or is it a funny way of saying too much? The words around it tell you which.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Overdose» means too much medicine or drug taken at once, which can make someone very ill or kill them.
fact: «To overdose» means to take a dangerous amount of medicine or a drug.
fact: Informal use: «an overdose of sugar» or «an overdose of TV» means far too much of something, and nobody is hurt.
fact: Only take medicine that an adult gives you, as the label says. If you think someone has taken too much, tell an adult straight away and call your emergency number.
exercise: «Even shop medicines such as cough medicine can be dangerous in big amounts, and any overdose is an emergency.» What is an overdose? | *Too much medicine or drug taken at once | A medicine that is sold in shops | A kind of cough | A small spoon of medicine
exercise: Mum says, «Ten more minutes on the tablet? That's an overdose of screen time!» What does overdose mean in this joke? | *Far too much screen time | Turning the screen off | A short break from the screen | A dangerous amount of medicine
exercise: Which is the right first step if someone may have taken too much medicine? | Hide the medicine box | Wait and see if it passes by itself | Give them more medicine so they feel better | *Tell an adult straight away and call your emergency number
test: What does it mean to overdose? | To take a small amount of medicine | To feel tired after a good sleep | *To take a dangerous amount of medicine or a drug | To forget to take your medicine
test: «Only take medicine an adult gives you, as the label says.» Why is this rule important? | Labels are only for decoration | Medicine works better if you take extra | *Too much medicine can make you very ill | Medicine tastes nicer when it is shared
exam: In «an overdose of homework», what does overdose mean? | A dangerous amount of medicine | Homework that is very easy | Homework finished in a hurry | *Far too much homework
